1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,052
The idea, preposterous.

2
00:00:02,053 --> 00:00:09,280
The plan, carve a national monument out of the solid black hills of South Dakota.

3
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:14,440
The motivation, part tourism, part patriotism, part egotism.

4
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:23,120
The result, a stunning monument to four American visionaries by a man as energetic and contentious as the country he loved.

5
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:28,560
Now, sculpting Mount Rushmore, here on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The End

7
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:56,000
After four long years of fighting, World War I ended.

8
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,340
In the 1920s, with the return of American troops,

9
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:02,940
came a national exuberance, a joyous jazz spirit

10
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:07,120
that seemed to encourage expansive, uninhibited behavior.

11
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,680
After all, we had won a war.

12
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:11,780
We could do anything.

13
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,220
It was time to celebrate.

14
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:18,860
We embraced great heroes and great hooligans.

15
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:22,020
There were those who would legislate morality

16
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:24,720
and those who would throw caution to the wind

17
00:01:24,721 --> 00:01:28,800
wind to reach dizzying new heights.

18
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:31,260
No one could predict the Great Depression,

19
00:01:31,261 --> 00:01:34,540
but it was only a few years away.

20
00:01:34,541 --> 00:01:37,140
American industry was retooling from the war

21
00:01:37,141 --> 00:01:41,000
economy to accommodate a rapidly growing consumer market.

22
00:01:41,001 --> 00:01:44,040
Mass production made new products available and affordable,

23
00:01:44,041 --> 00:01:46,140
particularly automobiles.

24
00:01:46,141 --> 00:01:49,560
Henry Ford was grinding them out at a rate of nearly 20,000

25
00:01:49,561 --> 00:01:51,120
a year.

26
00:01:51,121 --> 00:01:53,480
The new American middle class was riding to work

27
00:01:53,481 --> 00:01:55,680
and turning the traditional family vacation

28
00:01:55,681 --> 00:01:57,720
into a great adventure on the road.

29
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:02,080
The money-making potential of this was not lost

30
00:02:02,081 --> 00:02:06,020
on Joan Robinson, 67-year-old state historian

31
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:08,420
and poet laureate of South Dakota.

32
00:02:08,421 --> 00:02:12,260
He longed to bring these people to the beauty of his state.

33
00:02:12,261 --> 00:02:15,300
But how do you convince tourists to drive for days

34
00:02:15,301 --> 00:02:18,500
on primitive highways to visit a forsaken corner

35
00:02:18,501 --> 00:02:21,040
of a largely undeveloped wilderness?

36
00:02:21,041 --> 00:02:23,740
Robinson claimed he had an illuminating flash,

37
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:28,140
an inspiration to distinguish South Dakota with a roadside attraction

38
00:02:28,141 --> 00:02:31,520
so unique that tourists would be compelled to visit.

39
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,660
He thought what would be great is if they'd carve some Western folk heroes,

40
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,440
Chief Red Cloud, Kit Carson, other folk heroes.

41
00:02:40,441 --> 00:02:43,180
If they could carve those in the Black Hills in the Needles District,

42
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,040
that tourists would come for miles to see them.

43
00:02:46,041 --> 00:02:49,440
The needles are ancient stone structures,

44
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:51,980
eroded stubs of granite mountains.

45
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,920
There are literally thousands of them in the Black Hills.

46
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:58,620
They're rough, uneven, impossible to carve.

47
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,220
But that was not enough to deter Doan Robinson.

48
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:06,920
All he really needed, he felt, was an artist who loved a challenge.

49
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:13,160
That man was Gutsen Borglum, a renowned stone sculpture.

50
00:03:13,161 --> 00:03:16,760
Gutson was always looking for new worlds to conquer.

51
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:23,100
Gutson believed that he was the best artist who has ever existed.

52
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,200
Borglum enjoyed working on an exaggerated scale.

53
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:30,280
He once lamented there was no monument in America

54
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,120
as big as the nation's spirit.

55
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:34,840
He had done big work.

56
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:37,560
He had designed a mountain carving in 1915

57
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:40,120
at a Georgia site called Stone Mountain.

58
00:03:40,121 --> 00:03:45,120
Now, South Dakota offered Borglum a new challenge.

59
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:49,120
The Rapid City Commercial Club had backed Robinson's concept,

60
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,560
basically said to Borglum, hey, find a mountain,

61
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:54,360
pick a subject, go to work.

62
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:56,880
We want a mountain carving.

63
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:00,880
In 1925, with a frontier guide and his son Lincoln,

64
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:04,720
Borglum explored the Black Hills for a carving site

65
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:06,720
that would suggest a theme big enough

66
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:10,120
for his bombastic reputation.

67
00:04:10,121 --> 00:04:13,120
He found both at Mount Rushmore, where he envisioned

68
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:16,120
the ultimate American monument.

69
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:19,960
Unlike the needles, Mount Rushmore was solid granite.

70
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:22,660
Even though the rock was riddled with minute cracks,

71
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:25,660
Borglum became fixated on the site.

72
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,780
Here is the place, he declared,

73
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:31,880
American history shall march along that skyline.

74
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:33,780
Then his men quickly scaled the peak

75
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:36,480
and claimed the site as a national monument.

76
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:38,580
He had no authority to do that,

77
00:04:38,581 --> 00:04:39,820
But that didn't stop him.

78
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:47,860
It was here Gutzon Borglum would labor for the next 16 years

79
00:04:47,861 --> 00:04:51,560
before he would die, trying to make his dream come true.

80
00:04:51,561 --> 00:04:54,400
He would revise his designs nine times,

81
00:04:54,401 --> 00:04:57,040
move a half a million tons of rock,

82
00:04:57,041 --> 00:05:00,440
and provoke some of the wildest political battles of the age.

83
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:12,580
Like the America he loved, Gutzon Borglum

84
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:14,580
came from hardy immigrant stock,

85
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:17,580
Danish Mormon seeking the New Jerusalem

86
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:20,520
in the barren wastes of Utah.

87
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:25,060
Much of his early work glorified the female form,

88
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:29,960
his female atlas, and conception.

89
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:32,040
As his work brought him recognition,

90
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:35,480
he moved into the world of the rich and powerful.

91
00:05:35,481 --> 00:05:41,960
he moved into the world of the rich and powerful and carefully cultivated extraordinary friends

92
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:52,440
the wright brothers he served as a timer on their early flights he campaigned for his friend teddy

93
00:05:52,441 --> 00:05:58,920
roosevelt's bull moose party he wrote social and political tracts that provoked outrage satisfying

94
00:05:58,921 --> 00:06:01,580
his appetite for confrontation.

95
00:06:01,581 --> 00:06:04,700
And in 1923, he even worked on a national committee

96
00:06:04,701 --> 00:06:07,660
trying to elect a Ku Klux Klan Grand Dragon

97
00:06:07,661 --> 00:06:09,360
to the White House.

98
00:06:09,361 --> 00:06:12,040
He got into the Ku Klux Klan because he saw it

99
00:06:12,041 --> 00:06:13,660
as a grassroots movement.

100
00:06:13,661 --> 00:06:16,400
They were a national organization.

101
00:06:16,401 --> 00:06:20,040
They had hundreds of thousands of members.

102
00:06:20,041 --> 00:06:23,680
And so for him, he was not really part of some kind

103
00:06:23,681 --> 00:06:26,120
of lunatic fringe group.

104
00:06:26,121 --> 00:06:30,820
He really thought that the Klan could be worked into an organization

105
00:06:30,821 --> 00:06:36,560
that would elect a president without the power bosses.

106
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,440
Borglum's crusades reflected his approach to art.

107
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,040
He insisted on working in monumental scale and on mythic characters.

108
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:49,080
You were separated in those days by whether you did or did not do a Lincoln.

109
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:53,920
All of the Lincoln artists were people on another level.

110
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,020
It was the big leagues.

111
00:06:55,021 --> 00:06:58,700
He named his son Lincoln so he could write the committees and say,

112
00:06:58,701 --> 00:07:00,360
remember, Lincoln is one of mine.

113
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:04,560
And he, of course, has done three of the best Lincolns,

114
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:06,860
acknowledged the best Lincolns in existence.

115
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:11,100
The one on Mount Rushmore, the seated Lincoln in Newark, New Jersey,

116
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,600
and the Lincoln portrait that's in the Capitol building

117
00:07:14,601 --> 00:07:16,460
and on the grave in Springfield.

118
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:21,280
It was ironic then that his biggest commission was the one in 1915,

119
00:07:21,281 --> 00:07:27,580
the attempt to make the South rise again through a mammoth tribute to the Confederacy, Stone Mountain.

120
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:33,940
Commissioned by the Daughters of the Confederacy, the work was designed to be as tall as a 20-story building.

121
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:36,420
But the project soon fell apart.

122
00:07:36,940 --> 00:07:40,880
In typical fashion, Borglum had a volatile disagreement with his patrons

123
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:46,740
who believed the artist was insulting the memory of General Lee by portraying him wearing a hat.

124
00:07:46,741 --> 00:07:53,640
In a fitful rage, he smashed his working models and fled Georgia with a lynch mob snapping at his heels.

125
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:58,720
Borglum needed another project and fast to restore his reputation.

126
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:06,400
His prayers were answered in 1925 when he received Doan Robinson's notes suggesting the massive work in South Dakota.

127
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:11,840
The backers of Mount Rushmore were able to say to Borglum time and time again,

128
00:08:11,841 --> 00:08:14,860
remember, Gutsen, you can lose one mountain.

129
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:18,860
You can't lose two without being historically a fool.

130
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:21,300
You must prove yourself here.

131
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:23,120
And he understood that.

132
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:27,420
A new mountain, a new dream, were what Borglum needed,

133
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:29,480
and Mount Rushmore offered both.

134
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:34,100
He quickly diminished Robinson's suggestion of Western heroes in stone

135
00:08:34,101 --> 00:08:37,680
and instead envisioned a tribute to American presidents

136
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:40,920
who symbolized the evolution of the American spirit.

137
00:08:40,921 --> 00:08:44,880
Washington, father of the country who led the fight for American liberty.

138
00:08:44,881 --> 00:08:49,860
Jefferson, author of the Declaration of Independence, whose vision of a growing

139
00:08:49,861 --> 00:08:55,200
nation inspired the Louisiana Purchase. Lincoln, the great emancipator who

140
00:08:55,201 --> 00:08:59,920
preserved the Union of States in its darkest hour. Teddy Roosevelt, the

141
00:08:59,921 --> 00:09:04,980
spirited individualist who defined the vibrant 20th century America and was an

142
00:09:04,981 --> 00:09:09,640
old friend of Borglum's. It would be two years before Borglum would carve the

143
00:09:09,641 --> 00:09:14,260
dream in granite. First he needed money and a president to go along with the plan.

144
00:09:14,261 --> 00:09:23,560
America's Jazz Age fell under the leadership of a president whose deadpan expression and

145
00:09:23,561 --> 00:09:29,180
flat personality were a far cry from speakeasies and flappers.

146
00:09:29,181 --> 00:09:32,080
Calvin Coolidge, a man not known

147
00:09:32,081 --> 00:09:35,420
for a wild sense of humor.

148
00:09:35,421 --> 00:09:38,620
But in 1927, in an act of providence,

149
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:41,320
he decided to vacation in South Dakota.

150
00:09:41,321 --> 00:09:43,460
Well, here was an opportunity.

151
00:09:43,461 --> 00:09:46,400
Borglum hoped to secure official federal recognition

152
00:09:46,401 --> 00:09:48,260
for his dream of Mount Rushmore,

153
00:09:48,261 --> 00:09:50,500
and maybe raise desperately needed funds

154
00:09:50,501 --> 00:09:53,040
to finally get the project underway.

155
00:09:53,041 --> 00:09:56,240
To impress the president, South Dakota Governor William Buleau

156
00:09:56,241 --> 00:09:59,380
christened a hunting lodge, the Summer White House.

157
00:09:59,381 --> 00:10:02,380
A local stream was renamed Grace Coolidge Creek,

158
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:05,420
and it was stocked with prize-winning trout and netted,

159
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:08,460
so the fish couldn't swim far from the presidential brook.

160
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:13,300
He hunted around and found some old Scandinavian with a big nose

161
00:10:13,301 --> 00:10:19,600
and employed him to sit on the bank of the Grace Coolidge Creek

162
00:10:19,601 --> 00:10:23,800
so that the fish would be accustomed to seeing a big-nosed person there

163
00:10:23,801 --> 00:10:27,280
and wouldn't be scared away when the Coolidge arrived.

164
00:10:27,281 --> 00:10:31,587
Well, the effort paid off. Coolidge began to loosen up.

165
00:10:31,588 --> 00:10:40,280
He began to wear hats, all kinds of hats, and Borglum was able to infect a president with Mount Rushmore fever.

166
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:47,900
On August 10, 1927, over 1,000 people showed up to catch a glimpse of Calvin Coolidge

167
00:10:47,901 --> 00:10:54,200
as he and the Secret Service arrived on horseback to mark the start of work on Mount Rushmore.

168
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:57,300
At the ceremony, Coolidge declared,

169
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,620
We have come here to dedicate a cornerstone laid by the hand of the Almighty,

170
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:05,320
and he handed Gutzon Borglum six ceremonial drill bits.

171
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:08,260
Not to be upstaged by the president,

172
00:11:08,860 --> 00:11:11,680
Borglum immediately scaled the 6,000-foot peak

173
00:11:11,681 --> 00:11:14,120
and had himself lowered over the heads of the crowd

174
00:11:14,121 --> 00:11:19,000
to begin carving, even though he hadn't actually worked out the entire design yet.

175
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:25,880
In Washington, as one of his last acts as president in 1929,

176
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:30,100
Coolidge signed the Rushmore Bill establishing matching federal funds

177
00:11:30,101 --> 00:11:32,100
for private donations to the monument.

178
00:11:32,101 --> 00:11:42,860
To some critics, it was either the most visionary act of his presidency or a comic coda to the decade of decadence that preceded America's plummet into the Depression.

179
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:55,300
Borglum's initial design featured only three figures, each rendered to mid-torso.

180
00:11:56,100 --> 00:12:01,820
His style was obsessively representational, relying on portraits, models, and life masks.

181
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:04,460
to capture every nuance of his subjects.

182
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:08,420
He began his designs as rough miniatures,

183
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:11,980
testing how they would respond to sunlight and shadow,

184
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:15,640
then reworking those into 30-foot-tall structures,

185
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:19,880
which still were only one-twelfth of what the finished work would be.

186
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:22,620
The model is made out of plaster,

187
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:25,460
and it also is made out of excelsior,

188
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,540
which was a packing material at that time.

189
00:12:27,541 --> 00:12:32,220
It has nails, it has pine needles, pine cones.

190
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:36,120
It was made from just about anything that Borglum had on stock at the time.

191
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:42,440
Borglum designed the eyes of his subjects to feature raised areas in the pupils,

192
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:44,760
suggesting a glint of light.

193
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:48,740
In Lincoln's eye, this required a 30-inch shaft of granite,

194
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:52,420
and he developed an almost impressionist style

195
00:12:52,421 --> 00:12:56,000
to render the spectacles that framed Roosevelt's face.

196
00:12:56,001 --> 00:13:01,000
In early films, Borglum describes the scope of the monument to Doan Robinson.

197
00:13:01,001 --> 00:13:06,000
And that is 60 feet in height, from the wig to the chin.

198
00:13:06,001 --> 00:13:11,000
Borglum, how large, from the pupils that I am, is about 3 feet in diameter.

199
00:13:11,001 --> 00:13:17,000
The upper lid is 18 inches. The head, as I told you, is 60 feet in height.

200
00:13:17,001 --> 00:13:22,000
The forehead is 20 feet in height. The nose is 20 feet in height.

201
00:13:22,001 --> 00:13:25,000
And from the nose to the end of the chin, another 20 feet.

202
00:13:25,001 --> 00:13:32,000
Even during the early stages, the effect was overwhelming.

203
00:13:32,001 --> 00:13:36,840
People don't realize that this head and the first waist part

204
00:13:36,841 --> 00:13:38,840
is as tall as the Niagara Falls.

205
00:13:38,841 --> 00:13:41,760
And that Washington might sit in the Niagara Falls,

206
00:13:41,761 --> 00:13:44,960
and the water would only break around his collar.

207
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:47,760
Washington's head alone is as large

208
00:13:47,761 --> 00:13:49,960
as the Sphinx in Egypt.

209
00:13:49,961 --> 00:13:55,000
The carving size dwarfs almost all ancient wonders.

210
00:13:55,001 --> 00:14:01,000
To transfer the designs onto stone, Borglum used an ancient technique called pointing.

211
00:14:01,001 --> 00:14:06,000
A circular plate measured in degrees is placed on top of the model.

212
00:14:06,001 --> 00:14:12,000
The degree which a boom points is recorded as well as the length of the boom out from the center of the plate,

213
00:14:12,001 --> 00:14:18,000
and how far down from the boom a string extends until it reaches an element of the design.

214
00:14:18,001 --> 00:14:25,000
These three measurements are multiplied by 12 and then duplicated on a larger pointing system on the mountain.

215
00:14:25,001 --> 00:14:30,000
This was the model that was used. It was a 1 to 1 12th scale.

216
00:14:30,001 --> 00:14:36,000
If you look onto the model itself, there's a boom that comes out and then a plumb bob.

217
00:14:36,001 --> 00:14:40,000
So one inch on the model would represent 12 inches on the mountain itself.

218
00:14:40,001 --> 00:14:47,000
Because of the high degree of accuracy needed, the pointer was the most important person on the mountain.

219
00:14:47,001 --> 00:14:51,240
In 1932, Borglum assigned his son Lincoln to that job.

220
00:14:51,241 --> 00:14:54,740
And from his vantage point, it was Lincoln who first recognized

221
00:14:54,741 --> 00:14:59,140
that the monument was quickly becoming more than just carving in stone.

222
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:27,520
It came to me in an almost terrifying manner, Borglum admitted,

223
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:30,140
that I had never sensed what I was planning.

224
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:33,000
Its dimensions, the vastness that lay here,

225
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:36,380
demanded the complete remodeling of the grouping I'd been dreaming.

226
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:41,380
As work proceeded, Borglum constantly had to change his design

227
00:15:41,381 --> 00:15:44,500
due to inconsistencies in the granite.

228
00:15:44,501 --> 00:15:52,920
His first attempt at a Jefferson head on Washington's right side had to be blasted away and a new one built on Washington's left.

229
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:56,100
Nature was participating in the design.

230
00:15:56,600 --> 00:16:03,180
When you look at Mount Rushmore, you'll notice that the Jefferson head is tilted back or seems to be looking up at a higher angle than the rest of the heads.

231
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:11,220
The romantic view of this is that Borglum saw Jefferson as a visionary, the person who saw where the country should go and knew how to take us there.

232
00:16:11,221 --> 00:16:15,060
reality was is that there was a crack that ran through Jefferson's nose they

233
00:16:15,061 --> 00:16:20,000
had to keep tilting it back until the crack no longer ran through the nose

234
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:26,400
Borglum needed a specially constructed aerial tram to survey the work as it

235
00:16:26,401 --> 00:16:32,300
progressed and instruct the crew on how to approach refinements we would see

236
00:16:32,301 --> 00:16:37,360
Borglum come up in the bucket and he'd stop the bucket out there in front with

237
00:16:37,361 --> 00:16:39,562
wind kind of moving him around.

238
00:16:39,563 --> 00:16:44,320
He'd look at either Lincoln or whichever one he had men working on.

239
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:51,360
He'd just look at it and then he'd go up and get some paint and a brush and go down and paint what

240
00:16:51,361 --> 00:16:55,402
he wanted. Nothing from here to three inches down to nothing.

241
00:16:55,403 --> 00:16:57,920
But then how he could make it come out

242
00:16:57,921 --> 00:17:04,800
looking like Lincoln and like Jefferson is amazing to me because it was so large when you got next to

243
00:17:04,801 --> 00:17:07,200
you just couldn't imagine it.

244
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:10,500
It was an incredible challenge to envision the monument

245
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:12,500
from such a close perspective,

246
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:15,340
but Borglum knew the figures were there.

247
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:19,640
It was just a matter of removing half a million tons of granite

248
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:22,120
to reveal them.

249
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:24,380
From the height of a 50-story building,

250
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:27,160
workmen were carefully lowered by iron winches

251
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:29,460
in special bosun chairs.

252
00:17:29,560 --> 00:17:31,960
Once the pointer verified a position,

253
00:17:31,961 --> 00:17:36,960
a workman used a pneumatic drill to create small holes for the dynamite.

254
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:39,960
For just enough dynamite...

255
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:45,960
to remove extra rock to within three inches of the finished surface,

256
00:17:45,961 --> 00:17:49,960
yet not enough to damage the area to be hand-carved.

257
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:58,960
In all, it was an exacting process, suggested to Borglum by European road builders

258
00:17:58,961 --> 00:18:01,600
who used the technique to blast holes in mountains

259
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:05,440
to build tunnels through the Alps.

260
00:18:05,540 --> 00:18:09,440
The more delicate work was called honeycombing the rock.

261
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:11,440
The surface of the stone was textured

262
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:13,940
with a close formation of drill holes.

263
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:17,120
Then smaller pieces of granite would be manually chipped off

264
00:18:17,220 --> 00:18:20,540
with a tool called the channel iron.

265
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:22,220
That's a channel iron.

266
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:23,920
They use it in the regular jackhammer

267
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:26,460
instead of a regular drill.

268
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:28,020
And then they cut between the holes

269
00:18:28,021 --> 00:18:32,260
holes so that you can wedge out pieces of rock without using powder.

270
00:18:33,260 --> 00:18:38,080
This channel iron, like the wide variety of steel drill bits used to shape the stone,

271
00:18:38,081 --> 00:18:40,200
was attached to a 60 pound jackhammer.

272
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:46,440
It operated on compressed air piped up from the base of the mountain, 1300 feet below.

273
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:51,640
And then the air would be funneled down into the jackhammer, which would make it go in and out.

274
00:18:51,641 --> 00:18:55,920
and then it would also, if I can lift it up here,

275
00:18:55,921 --> 00:19:00,320
the end has a hole in it which would allow the air to come through and then

276
00:19:00,321 --> 00:19:01,120
push the

277
00:19:01,121 --> 00:19:04,960
excess dust away. But when you were in a bosom chair

278
00:19:04,961 --> 00:19:09,520
with about a 40 mile an hour wind and hanging on a cable off the side of the

279
00:19:09,521 --> 00:19:10,221
mountain

280
00:19:10,222 --> 00:19:13,280
and it might be 20 degrees out, you had to hold that

281
00:19:13,281 --> 00:19:18,400
jackhammer away from your body and as they held it away

282
00:19:18,401 --> 00:19:20,400
It would of course wear them out.

283
00:19:20,401 --> 00:19:23,400
There's a lot of crooked drill holes up there.

284
00:19:23,401 --> 00:19:25,400
Donald blames me for that.

285
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:33,400
He couldn't drill straight.

286
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:38,400
Anytime you drill a crooked hole, you've got to cut it out with a channel iron.

287
00:19:38,401 --> 00:19:40,400
You don't get out of working.

288
00:19:40,401 --> 00:19:43,400
When they would use these jackhammers for any length of time,

289
00:19:43,401 --> 00:19:47,400
they would actually take their shoes and put them in the form of a V,

290
00:19:47,401 --> 00:19:49,900
and they would use that V to get the hole started.

291
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:52,560
And they would go through a pair of shoes

292
00:19:52,561 --> 00:19:53,980
by not wearing out the bottom,

293
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:57,760
but actually wearing the insteps out first.

294
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:01,480
Finally, pneumatic devices and hand tools

295
00:20:01,481 --> 00:20:03,320
were used to grind rough areas,

296
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:06,300
then polish the finish to a smooth surface

297
00:20:06,301 --> 00:20:08,440
in a process called bumping.

298
00:20:09,220 --> 00:20:10,980
This is a Dalit hand facer.

299
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:12,380
It also was run with air.

300
00:20:12,381 --> 00:20:13,800
Air would come into the back.

301
00:20:13,801 --> 00:20:21,560
the four-star drill bit was placed in the dowlet hand facer, but it wasn't locked in place.

302
00:20:22,340 --> 00:20:25,820
And the reason that he didn't lock it in place is because he didn't want it to drill a hole.

303
00:20:26,220 --> 00:20:28,420
All he wanted to do was bump the faces smooth.

304
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:34,080
So this was used, and it just went around and bumped the faces smooth just as though you would use sandpaper.

305
00:20:35,260 --> 00:20:41,220
He had to be pretty strong to do that all day, because that's pretty heavy, especially for an old man like me.

306
00:20:41,221 --> 00:20:48,560
When working so close to the enormous heads, it was difficult to have a sense of the finished

307
00:20:48,561 --> 00:20:49,560
design.

308
00:20:49,561 --> 00:20:54,660
So to help the men get the feel of the sculpture, Borglum had five-foot models of each head

309
00:20:54,661 --> 00:20:57,900
placed on top of the mountain to be used as guides.

310
00:20:57,901 --> 00:21:02,560
The workers talked about how big the faces were when you were right on the faces working

311
00:21:02,561 --> 00:21:03,560
up there.

312
00:21:03,561 --> 00:21:10,000
So he decided that what he would do was create these smaller life-size models.

313
00:21:10,001 --> 00:21:13,480
Then he would take them and transfer them right up on the mountain.

314
00:21:13,481 --> 00:21:18,780
So the workers could actually feel the inside of the nose and underneath the eyes.

315
00:21:18,781 --> 00:21:22,460
And they could actually tell where they were working and he could point out better, if

316
00:21:22,461 --> 00:21:25,380
you're going to go back this far, this is what you'll be doing and this is what it'll

317
00:21:25,381 --> 00:21:27,240
look like when you're done.

318
00:21:27,241 --> 00:21:31,820
We needed jobs, that was the main thing.

319
00:21:31,821 --> 00:21:38,500
I started out at 50 cents an hour and at that time we worked six days a week and every two

320
00:21:38,501 --> 00:21:43,098
weeks we got a check for $52 and that was really good money.

321
00:21:43,099 --> 00:21:45,780
At that time I was happy to get it.

322
00:21:47,460 --> 00:21:52,780
At that time in South Dakota unemployment was running at 34 percent.

323
00:21:52,781 --> 00:21:54,580
People needed the jobs,

324
00:21:54,581 --> 00:21:58,980
but experience in stone carving or hard rock mining were not necessary.

325
00:22:00,100 --> 00:22:04,563
Actually probably got the job because I was a good baseball player. Mr.

326
00:22:04,564 --> 00:22:06,260
Borgham's son Lincoln was a

327
00:22:06,261 --> 00:22:09,560
a big baseball fan.

328
00:22:09,561 --> 00:22:12,460
So Lincoln thought, well, I'll hire baseball players

329
00:22:12,461 --> 00:22:14,800
and we'll have a baseball team up here.

330
00:22:14,801 --> 00:22:16,800
The Mount Rushmore baseball team

331
00:22:16,801 --> 00:22:19,080
became a local institution.

332
00:22:19,081 --> 00:22:21,300
It went all the way to the state championship

333
00:22:21,301 --> 00:22:22,980
on several occasions.

334
00:22:22,981 --> 00:22:25,280
In fact, recruiting drillers and infielders

335
00:22:25,281 --> 00:22:27,780
was done simultaneously.

336
00:22:27,781 --> 00:22:29,020
I played shortstop.

337
00:22:29,021 --> 00:22:31,420
That's the reason I got the job.

338
00:22:31,421 --> 00:22:33,320
It was not surprising that these men

339
00:22:33,321 --> 00:22:35,460
would play well together on a team.

340
00:22:35,461 --> 00:22:39,600
They had a special bond, sharing the same demanding and dangerous task.

341
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:43,100
What is surprising is that in the 14 years of construction,

342
00:22:43,520 --> 00:22:45,640
there were no critical accidents or deaths.

343
00:22:46,280 --> 00:22:48,180
When you strapped yourself in one of those things

344
00:22:48,181 --> 00:22:51,260
and they dropped a boy down over that, he was pretty careful.

345
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:54,100
I remember at that time, just an example,

346
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:57,380
I smoked a pipe a lot at that time.

347
00:22:57,381 --> 00:23:02,140
And I had been silly enough to cut a hole in my respirator with a razor blade

348
00:23:02,141 --> 00:23:04,540
and had my pipe sticking through my respirator.

349
00:23:04,541 --> 00:23:09,240
when Lincoln came up one time and he saw me down there.

350
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:11,920
Right away, he comes right down.

351
00:23:12,020 --> 00:23:13,580
He has a respirator in his pocket.

352
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:17,640
He just went, of course, I didn't have the pipe when he'd come down

353
00:23:17,641 --> 00:23:18,960
because I see him coming.

354
00:23:20,140 --> 00:23:22,440
And he says, that'll be all of that.

355
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:25,560
In contrast to his imposing father,

356
00:23:25,800 --> 00:23:28,780
Lincoln Borglum seemed a silent partner on the project,

357
00:23:28,781 --> 00:23:33,880
yet he was most responsible for supervising progress and employee relations.

358
00:23:34,540 --> 00:23:35,240
Mr.

359
00:23:35,241 --> 00:23:43,140
Borglum's son Lincoln, he doesn't get any credit at all for the work he'd done, or at least very little.

360
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:46,300
And Lincoln, he was here all the time.

361
00:23:46,360 --> 00:23:47,060
Quit college.

362
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:49,525
And he worked up there, and Mr.

363
00:23:49,526 --> 00:23:53,980
Borglum was gone a lot of times for three, four months at a time.

364
00:23:53,981 --> 00:23:59,980
And Lincoln actually supervised the work that was being done.

365
00:23:59,981 --> 00:24:03,780
and he doesn't get much credit at all,

366
00:24:03,781 --> 00:24:06,460
and I think he deserves a lot of credit.

367
00:24:06,461 --> 00:24:09,580
Gutson was the exacting taskmaster.

368
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:12,200
Lincoln would often smooth over the hurt feelings

369
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:14,220
caused by his father.

370
00:24:14,221 --> 00:24:18,560
We didn't get too much contact with Mr. Borglum.

371
00:24:18,561 --> 00:24:21,000
We all knew him, spoke to him, and so forth,

372
00:24:21,001 --> 00:24:23,160
but we didn't get close to him.

373
00:24:23,161 --> 00:24:27,500
He was very quick to make decisions,

374
00:24:27,501 --> 00:24:30,100
and he wanted everything perfect.

375
00:24:30,101 --> 00:24:32,400
There was a lot of people there that,

376
00:24:32,401 --> 00:24:36,900
I know one of the drillers that was a finished driller,

377
00:24:36,901 --> 00:24:40,280
been fired five or six times on the weekend,

378
00:24:40,281 --> 00:24:42,020
and then Lincoln would come back

379
00:24:42,021 --> 00:24:44,560
and get him on Monday morning, see?

380
00:24:44,561 --> 00:24:47,120
But he was different.

381
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:51,220
Gutzon Borglum's reputation for confrontation

382
00:24:51,221 --> 00:24:52,760
had been firmly established

383
00:24:52,761 --> 00:24:55,760
before the Mount Rushmore project began.

384
00:24:55,761 --> 00:24:57,300
But with the onset of the Depression

385
00:24:57,301 --> 00:24:59,360
and the resulting shortage of funds,

386
00:24:59,361 --> 00:25:02,400
he found it ever harder to control his temper.

387
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:16,060
In 1929, when the stock market crashed,

388
00:25:16,061 --> 00:25:18,400
so did the American spirit.

389
00:25:18,401 --> 00:25:21,560
The Great Depression quickly swept across the United States,

390
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:23,740
entering a season of gloom.

391
00:25:23,741 --> 00:25:25,980
12 million people were out of work.

392
00:25:25,981 --> 00:25:28,920
bad times were of biblical proportions.

393
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:32,820
Families were torn apart as men combed the nation

394
00:25:32,821 --> 00:25:35,760
for any kind of work at any rate of pay.

395
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:39,760
And before long, dwindling resources caused work

396
00:25:39,761 --> 00:25:43,340
to become sporadic on Borglum's Mount Rushmore.

397
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:50,340
Gutzin could not understand how at Mount Rushmore

398
00:25:50,700 --> 00:25:54,140
anybody could tell him there isn't enough in the treasury

399
00:25:54,141 --> 00:25:56,380
for the next box of dynamite.

400
00:25:56,381 --> 00:25:59,700
He's carving stone that's going to be recognizable

401
00:25:59,701 --> 00:26:01,920
100,000 years from now.

402
00:26:01,921 --> 00:26:05,580
How can you tell him today's production must stop?

403
00:26:05,581 --> 00:26:09,880
He could never understand the gift he was leaving

404
00:26:09,881 --> 00:26:12,820
for the world being related to dollars.

405
00:26:14,300 --> 00:26:17,600
Funding for the monument was limited to nickels and dimes

406
00:26:17,601 --> 00:26:21,400
donated by school children and matched by the government.

407
00:26:21,401 --> 00:26:23,980
Major federal grant money was needed at a time

408
00:26:23,981 --> 00:26:27,080
when none was available, especially for such an odd

409
00:26:27,081 --> 00:26:28,920
and overwhelming project.

410
00:26:28,921 --> 00:26:31,820
But ironically, what had started as a tourist attraction

411
00:26:31,821 --> 00:26:35,440
in the wilds of South Dakota was becoming an important symbol

412
00:26:35,441 --> 00:26:39,280
of national pride in a period when pride was about all people

413
00:26:39,281 --> 00:26:40,360
had left.

414
00:26:40,361 --> 00:26:42,320
Millions throughout the world envious the privilege

415
00:26:42,321 --> 00:26:44,800
and right to call ourselves Americans.

416
00:26:44,801 --> 00:26:47,100
Let us prove our deep appreciation of that privilege

417
00:26:47,101 --> 00:26:49,720
by rededicating ourselves to true Americanism

418
00:26:49,721 --> 00:26:53,200
with faith in God, faith in man, faith in liberty,

419
00:26:53,201 --> 00:26:55,000
and faith in America.

420
00:26:56,060 --> 00:26:58,840
The Roosevelt administration's vigorous promotion

421
00:26:58,841 --> 00:27:01,140
of a revitalized sense of Americanism

422
00:27:01,141 --> 00:27:04,580
inspired Borglum with a new publicity strategy.

423
00:27:04,581 --> 00:27:07,880
It was clear that his relentless dedication to the project

424
00:27:07,881 --> 00:27:10,920
and the monument's reverence toward the democratic dream

425
00:27:10,921 --> 00:27:12,560
were perfect springboards

426
00:27:12,561 --> 00:27:15,760
for the patriotic government's support of Mount Rushmore,

427
00:27:15,761 --> 00:27:17,220
and Borglum knew it.

428
00:27:17,221 --> 00:27:19,200
Using his talent for self-promotion,

429
00:27:19,201 --> 00:27:22,320
Gutsen kicked off a series of theatrical dedications

430
00:27:22,321 --> 00:27:25,020
to mark each stage of the carving process

431
00:27:25,021 --> 00:27:27,500
and to keep the public and political eye focused

432
00:27:27,501 --> 00:27:28,500
on his dream.

433
00:27:29,820 --> 00:27:32,240
America's gigantic Mount Rushmore Memorial

434
00:27:32,241 --> 00:27:34,200
is vested by President Roosevelt,

435
00:27:34,201 --> 00:27:36,600
who comes to dedicate the Jefferson figure.

436
00:27:36,601 --> 00:27:39,460
Sculptor Gutzon Borglum's daughter gives the signal.

437
00:27:40,640 --> 00:27:42,440
That shot was fired just above

438
00:27:42,441 --> 00:27:44,540
what will finally be Jefferson's hand.

439
00:27:44,541 --> 00:27:54,400
Egypt's ferreals conceive nothing so spectacular as this enduring monument in South Dakota's

440
00:27:54,401 --> 00:27:59,213
Black Hills. The 65-foot Jefferson head alone is three times bigger than Sink.

441
00:27:59,214 --> 00:28:00,200
The four immense

442
00:28:00,201 --> 00:28:04,660
heads, Washington, Jefferson, Lincoln, and Theodore Roosevelt, when completed, will be

443
00:28:04,661 --> 00:28:08,860
the world's greatest patriotic memorial, America's heritage to future generations.

444
00:28:08,861 --> 00:28:12,860
Borglum loved a big show, but his grandest publicity scheme

445
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:14,860
was destined to strain friendships

446
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:16,860
and cost him a powerful ally.

447
00:28:23,860 --> 00:28:27,860
He loved America and wanted to proclaim it in letters 20 feet

448
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:28,860
tall.

449
00:28:28,960 --> 00:28:31,860
His original plan for Mount Rushmore included a giant

450
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:33,860
timeline of American history.

451
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:36,860
He called it the entablature.

452
00:28:37,740 --> 00:28:41,620
Calvin Coolidge had been selected to write the 500-word composition

453
00:28:41,621 --> 00:28:45,900
as a condition of federal funding in the 1929 Rushmore Bill,

454
00:28:46,240 --> 00:28:48,900
which Coolidge himself had signed into law.

455
00:28:50,020 --> 00:28:55,420
In 1930, the ex-president sent a rough draft of the composition to South Dakota.

456
00:28:56,020 --> 00:29:00,020
Borglum promptly edited it to suit his own vision of American history,

457
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:03,380
releasing it immediately to anxious reporters.

458
00:29:03,381 --> 00:29:08,120
Coolidge was shocked to see an altered version of his text published in newspapers.

459
00:29:08,121 --> 00:29:15,460
This is not what I wrote, he told Borglum, but it's what you meant to write, Borglum replied, and he began carving.

460
00:29:16,280 --> 00:29:21,100
The public was astonished at Borglum's audacity and the embarrassing tempest it inspired.

461
00:29:21,680 --> 00:29:24,560
It destroyed the friendship between the artist and Coolidge.

462
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:32,240
They never spoke again, and the entablature project was halted by Congress until it accurately reflected Coolidge's composition.

463
00:29:32,241 --> 00:29:38,180
In 1934, after Coolidge had died and Congress was distracted, an ingenious newspaper man

464
00:29:38,181 --> 00:29:43,420
saw an opportunity to generate publicity and sell papers with a new twist on Borglum's

465
00:29:43,421 --> 00:29:45,060
entablature.

466
00:29:45,061 --> 00:29:49,700
William Randolph Hearst conspired with Borglum on a national promotion.

467
00:29:49,701 --> 00:29:54,000
Who will write the epic of America?

468
00:29:54,001 --> 00:29:58,120
Here on Mount Rushmore, beside the colossal figures of Washington and other leaders, will

469
00:29:58,121 --> 00:30:00,800
be carved an eloquent summary of the nation's development.

470
00:30:00,801 --> 00:30:05,460
To this, the public is invited to contribute in a gigantic countrywide contest sponsored

471
00:30:05,461 --> 00:30:07,860
by the Hearst newspapers.

472
00:30:07,861 --> 00:30:11,720
Cash and other prizes go to the winners whose combined text would be chiseled in the solid

473
00:30:11,721 --> 00:30:15,120
rock by Goodson Borglund, famous sculptor.

474
00:30:15,121 --> 00:30:18,960
A mighty and permanent salute to the spirit of progress.

475
00:30:18,961 --> 00:30:27,560
Over a million people, historians and teachers and citizens and congressmen, all were writing

476
00:30:27,561 --> 00:30:34,300
histories for this problem and finding a suitable text.

477
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:39,420
In these rare outtakes from the newsreel that was never released, Borglum awarded the winning

478
00:30:39,421 --> 00:30:41,260
student authors.

479
00:30:41,261 --> 00:30:47,900
These three young men were awarded prizes in the successful competition, successful national

480
00:30:47,901 --> 00:30:51,780
competition.

481
00:30:51,781 --> 00:30:54,820
These three young men were awarded...

482
00:30:54,821 --> 00:30:57,420
God damn it, you should have given me the right one.

483
00:30:57,421 --> 00:31:00,860
I met the challenge and I wrote it the way I thought it should be,

484
00:31:00,861 --> 00:31:03,920
and I still believe what I read from others

485
00:31:03,921 --> 00:31:05,960
and the history of our country,

486
00:31:05,961 --> 00:31:08,760
that it did the best I could at the time.

487
00:31:08,761 --> 00:31:13,000
In the end, however, Burkitt's words were never carved in stone.

488
00:31:13,001 --> 00:31:15,340
The idea had always been impractical.

489
00:31:15,341 --> 00:31:17,700
There wasn't enough stone for the full text.

490
00:31:17,701 --> 00:31:21,780
Borglum envisioned an etablature where the Lincoln figure is today.

491
00:31:21,781 --> 00:31:24,740
And then when he had to move the Jefferson figure over

492
00:31:24,741 --> 00:31:26,820
to the left of Washington,

493
00:31:26,821 --> 00:31:28,780
he also had to move the Lincoln figure.

494
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:32,500
So that took away where the entablature would have been.

495
00:31:33,400 --> 00:31:36,340
Borglum was disappointed that the entablature fell through,

496
00:31:36,341 --> 00:31:40,680
but this wasn't to be the only unfinished dream on the mountain.

497
00:31:41,880 --> 00:31:45,160
I think that we can perhaps meditate a little

498
00:31:45,161 --> 00:31:49,740
on those Americans 10,000 years from now

499
00:31:49,741 --> 00:31:57,000
when the weathering of the face of Washington and Jefferson and Lincoln

500
00:31:57,001 --> 00:32:01,440
shall have proceeded to a depth of perhaps a tenth of an inch.

501
00:32:03,560 --> 00:32:06,100
Ten thousand years from now,

502
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:10,300
I think we can meditate and wonder whether our descendants,

503
00:32:10,301 --> 00:32:12,320
because I think they'll still be here,

504
00:32:13,900 --> 00:32:16,140
what they will think about us,

505
00:32:16,141 --> 00:32:21,920
and let us hope that at least they will give us the benefit of the doubt.

506
00:32:22,780 --> 00:32:27,440
That they will believe that we have honestly striven in our day and generation

507
00:32:27,441 --> 00:32:33,660
to preserve for our descendants a decent land to live in

508
00:32:33,661 --> 00:32:37,640
and a decent form of government to operate under.

509
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:42,220
10,000 years from now, the faces of Mount Rushmore may still be there,

510
00:32:42,221 --> 00:32:46,540
But will anyone remember who these giants were or the people who honored them?

511
00:32:47,260 --> 00:32:52,660
Borglum tackled that problem by designing a hall of records to be drilled in the granite behind the heads

512
00:32:52,661 --> 00:32:59,480
to house copies of the Constitution, a history of the United States, and busts of 24 famous Americans.

513
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:04,620
The plan was the hall would be carved behind Lincoln's head.

514
00:33:05,420 --> 00:33:09,400
There would be a tunnelway extending 100 feet into the stone,

515
00:33:09,401 --> 00:33:15,280
leading visitors into an enormous domed room that would house historic artifacts.

516
00:33:15,780 --> 00:33:18,840
I would never have worked in there if I'd have starved to death.

517
00:33:19,420 --> 00:33:20,660
They were drilling dry.

518
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:24,140
You couldn't recognize them when they come out until you blew them off.

519
00:33:24,840 --> 00:33:27,580
That dust is terrible.

520
00:33:28,340 --> 00:33:29,860
It was a bad situation.

521
00:33:30,720 --> 00:33:35,540
In many ways, carving the Hall of Records was more difficult than sculpting the portraits.

522
00:33:35,541 --> 00:33:39,040
Work on the Hall of Records ended quickly after Borglum's

523
00:33:39,140 --> 00:33:41,180
death in 1941.

524
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:45,320
World War loomed, and Congress wanted the monument finished

525
00:33:45,420 --> 00:33:46,520
fast.

526
00:33:46,620 --> 00:33:49,660
I believe that Mr. Borglum was a real visionary.

527
00:33:49,760 --> 00:33:54,320
He wanted no chance that this monument to America,

528
00:33:54,420 --> 00:33:57,260
to freedom and democracy, would be lost

529
00:33:57,360 --> 00:33:59,460
to our future generations.

530
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:03,540
And he wanted to ensure that we had that record

531
00:34:03,541 --> 00:34:09,080
record in a secure environment, and that was to be the Hall of Records.

532
00:34:09,081 --> 00:34:15,000
The original plans were to have a tunnel way into the mountain about 100 feet and then

533
00:34:15,001 --> 00:34:17,820
a very large domed out room.

534
00:34:17,821 --> 00:34:22,360
We have a tunnel way into the mountain of about 68 feet that was completed by the workers

535
00:34:22,361 --> 00:34:24,160
before work was halted.

536
00:34:24,161 --> 00:34:28,960
Well, I think the numbers were placed there by Gutz and Borglum to indicate the depth

537
00:34:28,961 --> 00:34:30,600
at which drilling needed to occur.

538
00:34:30,601 --> 00:34:36,320
In this case, I'd say it looks like a depth of 12 inches was indicated.

539
00:34:36,321 --> 00:34:41,780
And then the blasting that would take it off to a cleaner level consistent with the depth

540
00:34:41,781 --> 00:34:43,500
of the honeycombing.

541
00:34:43,501 --> 00:34:48,600
And then this blasted level had yet to be finally finished to get to the relatively

542
00:34:48,601 --> 00:34:53,560
smooth process we see on the faces or outside the Hall of Records.

543
00:34:53,561 --> 00:34:58,360
And in the Hall of Records, you can see the further in you go, the rougher or earlier

544
00:34:58,361 --> 00:35:01,760
on in that entire process that the workers were.

545
00:35:01,860 --> 00:35:04,500
Because it wasn't finished, what is preserved

546
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:08,500
illustrates the process of construction.

547
00:35:08,600 --> 00:35:11,000
You do feel like you're very much standing

548
00:35:11,100 --> 00:35:13,400
on the doorstep of history at the Hall of Records.

549
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:17,420
And part of the reason, I think, is because it is this unfinished

550
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:19,720
dream concept that we see here.

551
00:35:19,721 --> 00:35:20,421
here.

552
00:35:27,900 --> 00:35:31,960
The mountain and the man, their destinies linked,

553
00:35:31,961 --> 00:35:34,440
spirits melded as one.

554
00:35:34,441 --> 00:35:38,040
Overimposing, overstated, larger than life,

555
00:35:38,041 --> 00:35:42,720
both possessed of an inner grace, both grand representations

556
00:35:42,721 --> 00:35:44,380
of the American spirit.

557
00:35:44,381 --> 00:35:47,040
So it seemed beyond belief that one could exist

558
00:35:47,041 --> 00:35:48,320
without the other.

559
00:35:48,321 --> 00:35:53,320
Yet on March 6, 1941, Gutzon Borglum died at the age of 73.

560
00:36:02,820 --> 00:36:05,280
Without the chief to bully and boast,

561
00:36:05,281 --> 00:36:07,780
activity on the monument seemed to enter a state

562
00:36:07,781 --> 00:36:11,020
of suspended animation, incomplete,

563
00:36:11,021 --> 00:36:14,920
stuck in a moment of time between dreams and reality,

564
00:36:14,921 --> 00:36:17,180
between promise and power.

565
00:36:17,181 --> 00:36:21,840
its full vision still frozen under countless tons of ageless granite.

566
00:36:25,080 --> 00:36:31,260
At the beginning of World War II, the United States had larger issues to consider.

567
00:36:31,720 --> 00:36:35,280
Gutzon's son, Lincoln, was commissioned to stop the work and clean up the site.

568
00:36:36,020 --> 00:36:37,500
Tools were disassembled.

569
00:36:37,940 --> 00:36:41,820
Borglum's models packed away and Lincoln climbed the peak one last time

570
00:36:41,821 --> 00:36:44,940
to say goodbye to his father's dream.

571
00:36:47,180 --> 00:36:59,640
The models which my father and I had made and changed so many times showed the figures

572
00:36:59,641 --> 00:37:01,460
were to be executed down to the waist.

573
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:04,920
I felt they were just as effective the way they are today.

574
00:37:05,780 --> 00:37:11,020
On my final report to the commission, I stated that the granite stairway and the hall of

575
00:37:11,021 --> 00:37:13,200
records were essential to the memorial's completion.

576
00:37:13,201 --> 00:37:20,540
I felt that the memorial would become a riddle to people a few thousand years from now without explanatory data.

577
00:37:21,540 --> 00:37:27,380
Today, the official view is that the memorial is finished and there are definitely no plans for more carving.

578
00:37:28,160 --> 00:37:31,640
Actually, there is no stone on Rushmore for another face.

579
00:37:32,460 --> 00:37:37,520
I do feel that the great symbolic message of the memorial is there.

580
00:37:39,240 --> 00:37:42,120
Many people wonder about should Mount Rushmore ever be completed.

581
00:37:42,121 --> 00:37:43,900
I think the answer to that is no.

582
00:37:44,360 --> 00:37:46,200
Certainly more work could have been done on it,

583
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:50,420
but there's a story to be told in unfinished dreams as well as finished dreams.

584
00:37:50,780 --> 00:37:52,300
It's a piece of art in its own right.

585
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:55,860
You know, it's one man's conception of the birth and growth of our country

586
00:37:55,861 --> 00:38:00,460
and what these four presidents represented to the first 150 years of our country.

587
00:38:01,080 --> 00:38:03,360
And changing or adding a head to Mount Rushmore now

588
00:38:03,361 --> 00:38:06,500
would be tantamount to, if you will, changing the smile on the Mona Lisa.

589
00:38:07,100 --> 00:38:10,120
As a man grows old, so does a mountain.

590
00:38:10,121 --> 00:38:16,360
The Black Hills themselves are old men, granite stubs left from the erosion of stone giants

591
00:38:16,361 --> 00:38:18,000
tens of millions of years ago.

592
00:38:18,520 --> 00:38:20,600
Their erosion continues today.

593
00:38:21,180 --> 00:38:27,600
The granite in the Black Hills is full of cracks, and the mountain itself has dozens.

594
00:38:27,780 --> 00:38:29,180
This is a natural crack.

595
00:38:29,980 --> 00:38:35,840
The primary thing we would do is to try to keep the moisture out of them, because the

596
00:38:35,841 --> 00:38:38,500
The biggest concern we'd have is moisture getting in,

597
00:38:38,501 --> 00:38:41,980
freezing and thawing cycles, and an actual wedging process

598
00:38:41,981 --> 00:38:45,420
whereby over many hundreds or thousands of years,

599
00:38:45,421 --> 00:38:48,760
that wedging process could cause some problems.

600
00:38:48,761 --> 00:38:51,120
While we know there's no imminent danger

601
00:38:51,121 --> 00:38:53,360
for the mountain itself, we do know that there's at least

602
00:38:53,361 --> 00:38:55,660
three cracks that pose future problems

603
00:38:55,661 --> 00:38:57,200
in the stability of the mountain.

604
00:38:57,201 --> 00:38:59,860
One of those cracks is in Lincoln's left eye,

605
00:38:59,861 --> 00:39:01,760
another is in Roosevelt's hairline,

606
00:39:01,761 --> 00:39:03,900
and another is in Washington's right ear.

607
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:17,400
This crack runs all the way through the left side of Washington's head and intersects with

608
00:39:17,401 --> 00:39:20,590
another crack coming in on a lateral plane.

609
00:39:20,591 --> 00:39:23,780
So we have a huge block of granite and it's

610
00:39:23,781 --> 00:39:26,712
not going anywhere. It weighs many, many tons.

611
00:39:26,713 --> 00:39:29,580
We've been measuring the spread of this crack

612
00:39:29,581 --> 00:39:32,400
with three pins that are placed.

613
00:39:32,401 --> 00:39:36,340
And about every month or so, somebody from our staff

614
00:39:36,341 --> 00:39:38,620
takes a measurement for the last 10 years,

615
00:39:38,621 --> 00:39:41,960
and we have had no detectable spread on this crack.

616
00:39:41,961 --> 00:39:44,200
The material that's patching this crack

617
00:39:44,201 --> 00:39:47,280
is the mixture that Gutz and Borglum used,

618
00:39:47,281 --> 00:39:49,440
which is the mixture of white lead, granite dust,

619
00:39:49,441 --> 00:39:51,040
and linseed oil.

620
00:39:51,041 --> 00:39:54,060
The substance that we've patched these cracks with most

621
00:39:54,061 --> 00:39:58,700
recently is a silicone-based, somewhat pliable substance

622
00:39:58,701 --> 00:40:03,700
that can expand and contract at this rate with the rock.

623
00:40:07,520 --> 00:40:12,100
The eerie silence of the Black Hills draws seekers still.

624
00:40:12,101 --> 00:40:15,620
It raises questions that can't be answered.

625
00:40:15,621 --> 00:40:18,420
Is there such a thing as eternity?

626
00:40:18,421 --> 00:40:21,400
Or is forever a moment we hold in our hearts?

627
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:34,300
Carrie in 1933 when I was a little girl, we used to come up here.

628
00:40:34,301 --> 00:40:38,640
I lived in Hot Springs and I came up here on Sundays or during the week and Borglum

629
00:40:38,641 --> 00:40:42,320
would talk to us and we watched Borglum carve those.

630
00:40:42,321 --> 00:40:47,820
There used to be stairs that came in behind Abraham Lincoln and you could walk up through

631
00:40:47,821 --> 00:40:48,820
there.

632
00:40:48,821 --> 00:40:53,080
They weren't restrictive like they are today and you could get up on top of there when

633
00:40:53,081 --> 00:40:54,080
they weren't working.

634
00:40:54,081 --> 00:40:58,960
And it was so amazing to me that anybody could actually

635
00:40:59,060 --> 00:41:01,260
carry out a dream like that.

636
00:41:01,360 --> 00:41:04,560
The Mount Rushmore Visitor Center welcomes over 2 and 1

637
00:41:04,660 --> 00:41:08,260
half million tourists a year, some reliving a childhood

638
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:13,600
experience, others on a patriotic pilgrimage.

639
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:16,060
As the public comes to the memorial

640
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:18,000
and they walk out on the terrace for the first time,

641
00:41:18,100 --> 00:41:20,940
if they've never seen it, I think they first ask how it was

642
00:41:21,040 --> 00:41:22,000
done.

643
00:41:22,100 --> 00:41:24,080
But after they've stood there a while and thought about

644
00:41:24,081 --> 00:41:27,220
about how, I think then they start to reflect on why.

645
00:41:27,221 --> 00:41:30,780
And the why is very important because it explains America

646
00:41:30,781 --> 00:41:32,480
and what makes America great.

647
00:41:33,720 --> 00:41:36,700
Mount Rushmore is the work of a dreamer

648
00:41:36,701 --> 00:41:39,480
inspired by a nation of dreamers.

649
00:41:39,481 --> 00:41:42,460
It was one man's vision, but it demanded the sweat

650
00:41:42,461 --> 00:41:45,640
and sacrifice of many ordinary men and women

651
00:41:45,641 --> 00:41:48,140
who would chisel their history in stone

652
00:41:48,141 --> 00:41:51,440
and celebrate the promise of the land they loved.

653
00:41:51,441 --> 00:41:54,500
There was a group of people that turned to be a family

654
00:41:54,501 --> 00:41:56,260
that worked up here.

655
00:41:56,261 --> 00:41:57,880
And that was always amazing to me.

656
00:41:57,881 --> 00:42:00,160
They never got much credit.

657
00:42:00,161 --> 00:42:01,940
They got very little credit.

658
00:42:01,941 --> 00:42:03,140
When they took a picture of them,

659
00:42:03,141 --> 00:42:06,120
nobody even took time to name them.

660
00:42:06,121 --> 00:42:08,520
But I'll tell you, I would like to see them named,

661
00:42:08,521 --> 00:42:11,100
and so would some of their widows.

662
00:42:11,101 --> 00:42:14,260
Those old ones that died from working up here

663
00:42:14,261 --> 00:42:16,960
all those years with the dust in their lungs and that,

664
00:42:16,961 --> 00:42:18,260
somebody ought to name them.

